From: =?UTF-8?q?Fran=C3=A7ois=20Roux?= <info@humanlearning.ch>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 21:05:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] rtc: pm8xxx: Add a static device-tree offset fallback
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

pm8xxx_rtc_probe_offset() has two sources for the epoch offset: an nvmem
cell, then a UEFI variable behind "qcom,uefi-rtc-info". When the machine
has no nvmem cell and efivars never register, the UEFI branch returns
-EPROBE_DEFER unconditionally while CONFIG_EFI=y, with no give-up path,
so the RTC never registers at all.

That is the situation on a Surface Pro 12in (X1P42100) booted at EL2 via
slbounce. After Secure Launch, TrustZone still answers a version query
(TZ_OWNER_SIP / INFO_VERSION returns 0x1402000) but refuses the trustlet
manager (TZ_OWNER_QSEE_OS / APP_LOOKUP fails with -22), so
qcom_qseecom_uefisecapp never probes, efivars never register and there is
no way to read RTCInfo. The same kernel at EL1 mounts efivarfs with 110
variables and the RTC works.

Add "qcom,rtc-offset" as a third source, tried after nvmem and before
UEFI. The offset is (unix time - raw counter), read off an EL1 boot where the
UEFI branch works.

It is NOT stable over time, and two earlier versions of this text were wrong
about how: the first called the offset constant, the second called it a
~250 s/day drift inferred from two measurements. Reconstructed boot by boot
over eight boots, it is a staircase, not a slope. The error moved 2.7 s across
5.5 days of uptime -- ordinary crystal drift -- while 7194 s were lost entirely
across power-off periods, and only long ones: six shutdowns of 17 s to 16 min
cost nothing, one of 1 h 55 min cost 1 h 55 min, as if the counter had stopped.
A fixed value is therefore good for a run, not across shutdowns. It is only
useful where something else corrects the clock shortly after boot; on this
machine NTP does, within a minute.

The static offset is deliberately read-only: with neither nvmem nor UEFI,
pm8xxx_rtc_update_offset() still returns -ENODEV, so a set-time cannot
silently overwrite it and then lose it at shutdown.

Also print the resolved offset at probe, which is how the value is
obtained in the first place: boot once at EL1, where the UEFI branch
works, and read it from the log.

Not sent upstream: the property is not documented in the binding, and the
underlying problem is arguably that the UEFI branch cannot give up. This
is published for people booting the same machine at EL2.

Tool assistance, per Documentation/process/generated-content.rst: the
diff, the commit message and the measurements quoted above were produced in a
long interactive session with an AI coding assistant. The human signer directed
the work, challenged the analysis repeatedly -- several intermediate conclusions
were wrong and were retracted after measurement -- ran every test on the
machine, and reviewed the result. No specialised analysis tool (coccinelle,
sparse, smatch) was used; the verification was empirical, on the hardware
described.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: François Roux <info@humanlearning.ch>
---
diff --git a/drivers/rtc/rtc-pm8xxx.c b/drivers/rtc/rtc-pm8xxx.c
index e624f848c..4d13e3ff1 100644
--- a/drivers/rtc/rtc-pm8xxx.c
+++ b/drivers/rtc/rtc-pm8xxx.c
@@ -575,6 +575,35 @@ static int pm8xxx_rtc_probe_offset(struct pm8xxx_rtc *rtc_dd)
 		return pm8xxx_rtc_read_nvmem_offset(rtc_dd);
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Static offset from the device tree. Third source, tried after nvmem
+	 * and before UEFI.
+	 *
+	 * At EL2 after a Secure Launch, TrustZone answers a version query but
+	 * refuses APP_LOOKUP (TZ_OWNER_QSEE_OS), so uefisecapp never probes and
+	 * efivars never register. The UEFI branch below would then return
+	 * -EPROBE_DEFER forever: there is no give-up path while CONFIG_EFI=y.
+	 *
+	 * The offset is (unix time - raw counter). Read it off an EL1 boot,
+	 * where the UEFI branch works, via the dev_info() in pm8xxx_rtc_probe().
+	 *
+	 * It is NOT stable over time, and it degrades as a staircase, not as a
+	 * slope. Measured over eight boots on a Surface Pro 12in: 2.7 s of error
+	 * gained across 5.5 days of uptime, but 1 h 55 min lost across a single
+	 * 1 h 55 min shutdown, as if the counter had stopped while powered off.
+	 * A fixed value is therefore good for a run, not across shutdowns, and
+	 * this is only useful where something else fixes the clock shortly after
+	 * boot. Here NTP does, within a minute; anything reading the clock
+	 * before that sees whatever every shutdown since has accumulated.
+	 *
+	 * Deliberately read-only: with neither nvmem nor UEFI,
+	 * pm8xxx_rtc_update_offset() still returns -ENODEV, so a static offset
+	 * cannot be silently overwritten by a set-time and then lost.
+	 */
+	if (!of_property_read_u32(rtc_dd->dev->of_node, "qcom,rtc-offset",
+				  &rtc_dd->offset))
+		return 0;
+
 	/* Use UEFI storage as fallback if available */
 	rtc_dd->use_uefi = of_property_read_bool(rtc_dd->dev->of_node,
 						 "qcom,uefi-rtc-info");
@@ -625,6 +654,10 @@ static int pm8xxx_rtc_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 		rc = pm8xxx_rtc_probe_offset(rtc_dd);
 		if (rc)
 			return rc;
+
+		/* Read this on an EL1 boot to learn the value for
+		 * qcom,rtc-offset; see pm8xxx_rtc_probe_offset(). */
+		dev_info(&pdev->dev, "offset = %u\n", rtc_dd->offset);
 	}
 
 	rc = pm8xxx_rtc_enable(rtc_dd);
